Use the appropriate supported pricing model.

Do not assume FlexiBill calculates actuarial premiums. Premium calculation should remain with the insurer unless a separately approved design states otherwise.

### Step 4 — Establish policyholder and policy references

Required data mapping:

| Field | Purpose |
|---|---|
| Policyholder ID | Customer-level identity |
| Policy number | Insurance contract reference |
| Premium billing ID | Unique premium obligation |
| Product code | Insurance product |
| Distribution channel | Direct or partner channel |
| Bank/partner code | Bancassurance mapping |
| Premium amount | Amount due |
| Billing period | Period covered by the premium |
| Due date | Scheduled collection date |
| Payment reference | Transaction correlation |

Use consistent, unique references across every billing cycle.

### Step 5 — Establish recurring billing

Define:

- Billing frequency.
- First billing date.
- Recurring billing date.
- Premium amount source.
- Subscription or recurring relationship.
- End condition.
- Payment authorization.
- Failure handling.
- Grace-period policy.

Validate whether the merchant should use a native subscription workflow, Account Billing, or an API/H2H integration.

The correct choice depends on who owns premium scheduling and billing obligation generation.

### Step 6 — Configure payment collection

Confirm the eligible payment methods for each market and insurance use case.

Validate:

- Recurring payment authorization.
- Token or mandate lifecycle, where applicable.
- Initial payment behavior.
- Subsequent collection behavior.
- Payment-method expiry or replacement.
- Failed-payment response.
- Transaction status and notification.
- Refund and reversal handling.

Do not assume all payment methods support recurring collection.

